She's like, yeah, it's just a grant. You're fine. Just take the money. But, you know, I graduated, I show it to my mom, she's like yeah, it's just a grant, you're fine, just take the money. But you know, I graduated, I went to graduate school, I became a professor, I paid off all my actual student loans, and when I had done so, I thought to myself, oh wait, I'm not done yet, there's that Hager award. And so I like, you know, go online and look it up. And I'm like, yeah, there it is. Like, I have a moral obligation to repay. I better start repaying this thing. And that letter, that line, that thing about how, you know, you have a moral obligation, it
她说，没错，这只是一笔补助金。没事的。拿着这钱就行。不过，你知道，我毕业了，我把它拿给我妈妈看，她说，没错，这只是一笔补助金，没事的，拿着这钱就行。不过，你知道，我毕业了，我去读了研究生，我成为了一名教授，我偿还了所有实际的学生贷款，当我做完这些后，我心想，等等，我还没完呢，还有那个黑格奖学金。所以我像，你知道的，上网去查了一下。然后我想，是的，它就在那儿。就像，我背负着偿还的道义责任。我最好开始把这笔钱还了。而那封信，那行字，关于你，你知道的，背负着道义责任的那句话，

5
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:37.000
caught me, it grabbed me and pulled me right in. The award made explicit something that's often overlooked in the way we think about money. At its core, money is about human relationships. And at a macro level, loans and gifts and paychecks and the dollars that come out of ATMs are clues to a culture, even the structure of a nation. I'd like to cash these nickels and I'll have them in quarters please. Thank you so much. On a good day it's about a half a million dollars. I'm a man. You got a dollar? Open your purse.
它击中了我，抓住了我，把我深深地吸引了进去。这个奖项明确表达了我们在看待金钱时经常忽视的一点。金钱的核心关乎人际关系。而在宏观层面，贷款、礼物、薪水以及从自动取款机里吐出来的美元，都是解读一种文化乃至一个国家结构的线索。我想把这些五分硬币兑换成零钱，请给我换成25美分硬币。非常感谢。在生意好的时候，大约有五十万美元。我是一个男人。你有零钱吗？打开你的钱包。

6
00:02:37.000 --> 00:03:36.000
I see you right there. Give it. Give me your dollar and I'll have you cash. Well that's right. You can go out and get yourself whatever you want. Hi dad. I just wanted to let you know that I'm just going to win the million dollars. Well, that's right. You can go out and get yourself whatever you want. Hi, Dad. Hi. I just wanted to let you know that I'm going to win the million dollars. This week on Hidden Brain, we examine the intangible elements of cold, hard cash. Coins and currency are not just tokens of economic value, but markers of emotional connection and shared history. There's a story we tell about what the world looked like before we invented money. It's a story built on the idea of butter.
我看到你了。给吧。把你的美元给我，我帮你兑换。好吧，就是这样。你可以出去买任何你想要的东西。爸爸，你好。我只是想让你知道，我要赢得一百万美元。好吧，就是这样。你可以出去买任何你想要的东西。爸爸，你好。嗨。我只是想让你知道，我要赢得一百万美元。本周的《隐藏的大脑》，我们来探讨冰冷、坚挺的现金背后那些无形的元素。硬币和纸币不仅是经济价值的符号，也是情感联系和共同历史的标记。对于在发明金钱之前世界是什么样子的，我们流传着一个故事。这是一个建立在物物交换构想之上的故事。

7
00:03:36.000 --> 00:04:07.000
This is the standard story that we read in the economics textbooks and that has been handed down to us at least since the mid-19th century if not before. This again is anthropologist Bill Morer, who told us the story of his moral obligation to repay that student loan. He's now the dean of the School of Social Sciences at the University of California Irvine, and he studies the origins and nature of money. And it goes something like this. In the beginning, before there was money, if I had something that you needed,
这是我们在经济学教科书中读到的标准故事，自19世纪中期甚至更早以来，它就一直代代相传。这位是人类学家比尔·莫雷，他刚才给我们讲述了他偿还学生贷款的道义责任的故事。他现在是加州大学尔湾分校社会科学学院的院长，他专门研究金钱的起源和本质。故事大致是这样的。在最初没有金钱的时候，如果我拥有你需要的东西，

8
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:29.000
I would approach you with that thing and see if you had anything that I needed. So if you wanted some fish and you needed some acorns and I had them, I could approach you with those acorns and we could basically barter on the spot, figuring out how many acorns equals how many fish. The problem with barter is it really does depend on us each meeting each other's needs in that moment.
我会带着那个东西去找你，看看你有没有我需要的东西。所以如果你想要一些鱼，需要一些橡果，而我正好有，我就可以带着这些橡果去找你，我们基本上可以在当场进行物物交换，算出多少橡果等于多少鱼。物物交换的问题在于，它确实取决于我们双方在那个当下能够满足彼此的需求。

9
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:48.000
And so the origin story of money that we have is that humans invented money essentially to be a mediator in these sorts of exchanges. In other words, if you, for example, had acorns, but I didn't actually want acorns, I wanted pots, for example, and I wanted to trade my fish for pots, and all you had to offer me was acorns,
因此，我们所听到的金钱起源故事是，人类发明金钱本质上是为了充当这些交易所的媒介。换句话说，如果你，比如说，有橡果，但我其实不想要橡果，我想要陶罐，比如我想用我的鱼换陶罐，而你唯一能提供给我的只有橡果，

10
00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:04.000
then essentially we couldn't negotiate. And economists sometimes have a term for this. They say that butter requires something called a double coincidence of wants. What is that? The double coincidence of wants essentially means that in that moment, I've got what you need. You've got what I need.
那么本质上我们就无法进行谈判。经济学家有时会用一个术语来描述这一点。他们说物物交换需要一种叫做“需求双重巧合”的东西。那是什么？需求双重巧合本质上意味着在那一刻，我有你需要的。你有我需要的。

11
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:30.000
There's a coincidence of our needs in the instance. And that rarely happens, right? So the idea here is that humans wizard up this kind of third thing, money, this magical substance that can put all things on one scale of value and use that as sort of their measuring stick, then, to make these kinds of transactions. So there's a problem with the story, and the problem is that it runs into some facts.
我们的需求在那个瞬间不期而遇。而这种情况很少发生，对吧？所以这里的想法是，人类凭空变出了这第三种东西——金钱，这种神奇的物质可以将所有事物放在同一个价值尺度上，然后以此作为他们的衡量标准，来进行这类交易。然而，这个故事有一个问题，问题在于它与一些事实相悖。

12
00:05:30.000 --> 00:06:14.000
I'm wondering if you can tell me about the work that you and other anthropologists have done, for example, in the Highlands of New Guinea, that call into question this origin story. The problem is that when we look around the world and in the historical and archeological record, for instances of this kind of direct barter, unfortunately, we don't find it. What we find instead really are systems where people are taking some kind of object, let's take a kind of a big shell valuable in the highlands of New Guinea, and using it to mark relationships. They're using it to mark relationships among people, among kin, that serve as a kind of indicator of enduring obligations that people have to one another.
我想知道您能否向我和听众讲讲，您和其他人类学家在例如新几内亚高地等地所做的工作，是如何对这个起源故事提出质疑的。问题在于，当我们放眼全球，在历史和考古记录中寻找这种直接物物交换的例子时，遗憾的是，我们找不到。我们真正发现的其实是一些系统，人们在其中利用某种物体——比如我们以新几内亚高地一种很大的贝壳贵重物品为例——来标记关系。他们用它来标记人与人之间、亲属之间的关系，这可以作为人们彼此之间存在持久义务的一种指标。

13
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:47.000
It's kind of like an open-ended IOU, right? If I give you this big shell thing, then that implies for us, but also for everyone around us, that we have a bond, we have a relationship that will essentially allow us to have a flow of goods between each other and between our families for the rest of our lives, essentially. So it's not so much that I'm giving you this shell so that you give me some pigs. It's I'm giving you this shell, so as to say, hey, someday I'm going to need some pigs. Some other day I might need your help in the field.
这有点像一张没有限定兑现日期的欠条，对吧？如果我把这个巨大的贝壳交给你，那么对我们而言，同时也对我们周围的所有人而言，这就意味着我们之间有了一种纽带，有了一种关系，它本质上能让我们在余生中，在彼此之间以及我们的家庭之间实现货物的流动。所以这与其说是我把这个贝壳给你，好让你给我一些猪，不如说是我把这个贝壳给你，意思是在说：“嘿，总有一天我需要一些猪。改天我可能需要在田里得到你的帮助。”

14
00:06:47.000 --> 00:07:21.000
Some other day you might need me to help you out with something. This shell kind of marks that relationship. It signifies to everyone around us that we have these ties of enduring obligation and responsibility. So in other words, it's not about a transaction, at least not in an immediate sense, it's about a transaction that perhaps unfolds over time more to do with obligation. My son is marrying your daughter, I'm giving you this precious necklace that I have and my expectation is over the next 20 years, our families are going to help one another in
改天你可能需要我帮忙处理某些事情。这种贝壳在某种程度上标志着这种关系。它向我们周围的所有人表明，我们拥有这种持久义务和责任的纽带。换句话说，这不关乎一笔交易，至少在直接的意义上不是，它关乎一笔也许随着时间推移而展开的交易，更多地与义务有关。我的儿子要娶你的女儿，我把这串我珍藏的项链送给你，我的期望是，在未来20年里，我们的家庭将在

15
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:38.000
all kinds of ways. That's right. It's really a memory device to indicate an ongoing enduring relationship, an ongoing set of obligations. And is some of this connected with the more basic idea, if you will, of just gift giving? When you think about gifts, part of what we're doing when I'm giving you a gift.
各种各样的事情上互相帮助。没错。这其实是一个记忆设备，用来表明一种正在进行的持久关系，以及一系列持续存在的义务。其中一些是否与关于“赠送礼物”的更基本概念有关？当你想到礼物时，当我们赠送礼物时，我们所做的一部分事情是：

16
00:07:38.000 --> 00:08:12.000
And presumably, this is true for people all over the world in different cultures. The gift is partly my telling you here something nice for you, but it also binds us in some kind of relationship that says down the line, you know, it's not explicit, but there's some implicit understanding that you're going to do something nice for me down the road. This is exactly how it works. Yeah, and the the early anthropologist Marcel Mos wrote about this quite explicitly saying that in the gift there's the obligation to give, there's the obligation to receive, but then also the obligation to reciprocate.
想必世界各地不同文化中的人们都是如此。礼物一方面是我对你表达“这里有些好东西送给你”，但它同时也把我们绑定在某种关系中，意味着在未来，你知道的，虽然没有明说，但有一种隐含的默契，即你在未来也会回报我一些好东西。这正是它的运作方式。是的，早期人类学家马塞尔·莫斯（Marcel Mauss）对此有过非常明确的论述，他指出，在礼物中包含着赠送的义务、接受的义务，但同时也有回赠的义务。

17
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:48.000
This obligation to reciprocate can be seen across human societies and across time. Some years ago, Bill stumbled on another interesting example of this. He was in Washington, DC, visiting a colleague who worked at the Smithsonian. She curated the museum's collections that relate to the history of money. Bill was with her inside a vault. And there's an area in the vault in a kind of long closet. And she and her interns call it Narnia because it's sort of like the the wardrobe and Narnia you go in and it goes on and on and on forever.
这种回赠的义务在整个人类社会和漫长的时间里随处可见。几年前，比尔偶然发现了另一个有趣的例子。当时他在华盛顿特区拜访一位在史密森尼学会工作的同事。她负责管理该博物馆与货币历史相关的藏品。比尔和她一起在一个金库里。金库里有一个区域，是一个长长的壁橱。她和实习生们把这里叫做“纳尼亚”，因为这有点像《纳尼亚传奇》里的衣柜，你走进去，它就没完没了地一直延伸下去。

18
00:08:48.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Inside this Narnia, there are boxes upon boxes of uncatalog artifacts. So I was there once with her and she said to me, do you want to go to Narnia? And I said, sure. So we go in, pull out a box at random, bring it out. She opens the box and there's a ton of these clay tablets covered in kuneiform and there's a sort of moment where both of us back away from the box. You know and I'm like oh don't touch anything don't break anything don't breathe.
在这个“纳尼亚”里面，摆放着一箱又一箱未经编目的文物。有一次我和她一起在那儿，她对我说：“你想去纳尼亚看看吗？”我说：“当然。”于是我们走进去，随机抽出一个箱子，把它拿出来。她打开箱子，里面有大量刻满楔形文字的泥板，那一刻我们俩都情不自禁地往后退了一步。你知道的，当时我就想：“天哪，别碰任何东西，别弄坏任何东西，甚至别呼吸。”

19
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:56.000
The clay tablets that they pulled out of the box were used 5,000 years ago in ancient Mesopotamia. They were used to keep records of obligations. When people lived in small groups, monitoring obligations was easy. I just remembered what you had said and done, and you remembered what I had said and done. But as human societies grew larger. People needed a way to systematically keep track of who owed what to whom. And we just can't keep track of all of that in our heads. What emerges essentially are systems of public administration,
他们从箱子里拿出来的泥板是5000年前古代美索不达米亚人使用的。它们被用来记录各种契约与义务。当人们生活在小群体中时，监督义务是很轻松的。我只记住了你说过的话和做过的事，你也记住了我说过的话和做过的事。但随着人类社会规模的扩大。人们需要一种方法来系统地追踪谁欠了谁什么。而我们单凭脑子根本无法追踪所有这些事情。随之应运而生的是公共行政系统，

20
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:23.000
where you've got centralized authorities who are responsible for organizing irrigation, organizing production so that you can have these larger scale settlements who set up systems of record keeping in the case that you mentioned in Mesopotamia using clay tablets so that people basically know what's going on, who's doing what, who owes what to whom. And you don't have the situation of something like a coin circulating from hand to hand
在这些系统中，有集权当局负责组织灌溉、组织生产，以便你们能够建立这些更大规模的定居点；他们建立了记录保存系统——就像你刚才提到的美索不达米亚使用泥板那样——好让人们基本了解发生了什么、谁在做什么、谁欠了谁什么。于是你不会遇到某种类似于硬币在人们手中流转的情况，

21
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:58.000
to pay your laborers, for example. Instead, you have these systems of tablets that are basically receipts, keeping track of things like labor, and then promising that, you know, after a certain period, if the labor has been done, the laborer is entitled to X. In a lot of our ancient Mesopotamian receipts, it's things like beer and grain and livestock. And it's just unbelievably magical to see these materials, and then to imagine the system that created them and the systems that they supported. It's really quite an incredible thing
比如用来支付工人的工资。相反，你拥有的是这些基本上属于收据的泥板系统，它们记录着劳动力等事务，并承诺说，你知道的，在经过一段时间后，如果劳动已经完成，劳动者有权获得X。在我们许多古代美索不达米亚的收据中，记录的都是啤酒、粮食和牲畜之类的东西。亲眼看到这些材料，然后想象出创造它们的系统以及它们所支持的系统，真的是不可思议的神奇。把自己重新带回那个世界，带回一个与我们今天生活的市场化社会系统如此截然不同的世界，去思考作为一个书吏，或者作为一个要求书吏写下这块泥板的人，又或者作为一个其整个人生都服从于某种生产组织形式的普通人，会是怎样一种体验，

22
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:22.000
to just put yourself back into that world, into a world that's so alien from the kind of system of market-based society that we live in today, and think about what it would be like to be that scribe, or to be the person who is, you know, asking the scribe to write this tablet, or to be, you know, the common person whose whole life is subjected to a kind of organization of production
这种基于这些书面记录、基于文字的集中行政管理系统，真的是一件相当了不起的事情。这并不是我们传统理解意义上的市场，对吧？这并不是那种人们聚集在一个地方，通过物物交换或货币来交换商品的理念。这更像是一种等级森严的组织系统，其本质是为了允许更大规模社会的创建。所以你刚才谈到了集中行政管理这个概念，从某种意义上说，这把我们带到了我们故事的另一个方面。我们刚才讨论了送礼和相互义务的概念。

23
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:59.000
and a system of centralized administration based on these written records based on writing. This isn't a market the way that we traditionally understand markets, right? It's not sort of the idea that people come together in a place and exchange goods either by barter or by money. This is much more a kind of hierarchical system of organization to essentially allow for the creation of these larger-scale societies. So you've talked about this idea of centralized administration, and in some ways this brings us to another aspect of our story. We've talked a little bit about the idea of gift-giving and mutual obligations.
我们讨论了收据、记录保存以及本质上将我们随着时间推移彼此约束的基本契约的概念。但同时也谈谈这些系统的中央管理理念，以及最终演变成监督整个系统如何运作的权威机构的引入。因此，要让货币发挥作用，你需要一种既是那种清算和记录保存系统，同时又是一种授权力量的东西，它实际上在宣告：这就是我们在这里保存记录的方式。所以，即使是在新几内亚高地，然后又在美索不达米亚，你都拥有这两者在共同发挥作用：记录保存系统和授权力量。

24
